[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me think about this problem.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$(\mathbb{Q}, +)$ is the additive group of rationals.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$(\mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)$ is the multiplicative group of nonzero [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrationals.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need onto (surjective) group homomorphisms $\phi: (\mathbb{Q}, +) \to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(\mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the structure of $(\mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mEvery nonzero rational $q$ can be uniquely written as $q = \pm \prod_p p^{n_p}$ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhere $p$ ranges over primes and $n_p \in \mathbb{Z}$ with only finitely many [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnonzero.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $(\mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}, \cdot) \cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z} \times [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\bigoplus_{p \text{ prime}} \mathbb{Z}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is because the sign gives a $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ factor, and each prime[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$p$ gives a $\mathbb{Z}$ factor (the exponent of $p$ in the factorization).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, $(\mathbb{Q}, +)$ is a divisible group. A group $G$ is divisible if for [0m
