[38;2;124;124;124mKey fact: Homomorphic images of divisible groups are divisible. So if $\phi: [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}$ is a surjective homomorphism, then $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}$ must be divisible (as a group under multiplication).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mIs $(\mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)$ divisible?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $\mathbb{Q} \setminus \{0\}$ to be divisible, for every $q \in \mathbb{Q} [0m
